00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.790
and see what is a service mesh.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:51.600
But a lot of enterprises either
considering or have already

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.400
started building
new services using

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:54.950
a microservices architecture.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.900
And we all know that
microservices are awesome.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.940
But why is that the case?

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.370
What was wrong with
the previous mechanism?

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:06.150
Well, this would be a
fairly good generalization

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.920
of a monolithic architecture.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.300
We've got some users.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.480
The front end business logic
is in a monolithic application

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.600
that connects to one
or more databases.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.540
Now this architecture
does has some advantages.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.610
You can then have a very
complex application,

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:21.780
those work just fine.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.770
It was easy from a
production point of view,

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:25.920
easy to troubleshoot.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.100
If you had latency issues, you
know exactly where to look.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:33.180
It's that one
monolithic application.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.690
And if you had to
deploy something,

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:37.620
you just deployed that
app, and you're done.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.440
But there are some
disadvantages to it as well.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.970
[INAUDIBLE] severely
impeded agility,

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:48.110
especially as that application
became more complex.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.090
The front team may be
making some changes.

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:52.610
The back end team may
be making changes.

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:55.730
Every change required to
test that entire monolithic

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:57.000
application.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.890
This is definitely a problem
and increased the ownership cost

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:02.660
for that application.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.540
And so we know
that microservices

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:07.640
solve that problem.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.950
So microservices
started decomposing

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:13.590
this monolithic
application, started

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:16.170
to decompose this
monolithic application

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:19.290
into independent units
functional units,

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:22.500
so that development
teams could operate

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.980
on those functional services
independent of each other.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:30.120
So in this example, that
monolithic application

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.090
was split into an invoicing
microservice, a front end

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:36.810
microservice, a NoSQL
DB cluster, and so on.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.840
And so if I were making changes
to the invoicing application,

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.060
I didn't have to involve
the front end team,

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.120
I could make those changes
and integrate much faster

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.930
without involving other teams.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:49.790
There are also some
interesting byproducts

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:53.120
from this architecture not
just related to agility.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.570
The first is that we could
use the right programming

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:58.730
language for our microservices.

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:02.060
The front end team could
use JavaScript NodeJS.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:05.660
The invoicing team could use
Springboot, .net, or something.

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:08.120
And they really didn't have--

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:10.410
didn't need to have
much in common.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:14.010
The second thing is that
as one microservices called

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.260
another microservices, i.e.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.720
when they transferred
responsibility

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.640
from one microservices
to the other,

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.870
they did it over the network.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:25.490
So a very common
pattern would be

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:29.720
that the invoicing microservices
exposed its interface as an API

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:32.870
over HTTP, and the front
end application didn't

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:35.660
have to know how it was
implemented, by whom it was

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.200
implemented, or anything as long
as that contact was maintained

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.330
[INAUDIBLE] the front end
application could invoke

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.120
the invoicing application.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.310
So this definitely
promoted agility.

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:49.280
It could introduce
changes much quicker.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.760
And this is definitely
a huge benefit

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:55.100
from the microservices
architecture.

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:58.850
Since people started adopting
more of these microservices,

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:01.730
they started making
them more fine-grained.

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:04.250
And before you know,
your architecture

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:07.320
ends up looking like this
because the more fine grained

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.380
you make it, the
more you can iterate

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:13.040
on one of those
individual microservices.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:17.060
Now this on the face of it
can look pretty daunting.

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:19.700
So what you're seeing
here is a network

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:23.290
of microservices that
make up one or more

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:25.170
monolithic applications.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:30.850
This network of microservices
is what we call a service mesh.

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:32.400
And as you can tell
us, as you build

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.710
more and more
microservices, this network

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:37.270
can become massive and complex.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:40.230
And this complexity becomes
very difficult to manage

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:41.700
and understand.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:44.880
If you put yourselves in the
shoes of a production operator

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.700
managing this sort of a
microservices architecture

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:51.210
and you had a performance
problem to identify,

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:55.410
it's going to be pretty
difficult to identify which

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:58.770
microservice actually was
causing the performance issue

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:00.720
because there are
so many services

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.030
running all at the same time.

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:06.800
Another issue is with
introducing agility

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.780
while keeping your risk low.

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:12.690
If the invoicing team is quickly
trading and making changes,

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:13.610
what if it breaks?

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:17.420
What if it pushed an inaccurate
change that broke a contract,

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:20.450
and now it causes a
cascading effect of breaking

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:22.510
other microservices?

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:24.220
And there's a third issue.

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:25.720
What about governance?

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:28.300
You have many teams
developing microservices

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:31.452
using programming
and platform choices

00:07:31.452 --> 00:07:33.160
that they want to make
that's best suited

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:34.780
for the microservice.

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:37.630
But then how does
a security admin

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:39.980
guarantee that
they're all adopting

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:41.920
best practices of security?

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:43.810
How do you know that
they're all adopting

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:46.970
the right standards for logging,
tracing, and everything else?

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:48.850
So there is a
governance issue now.

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:52.520
So we identified three
interesting things, right?

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.620
How do you observe?

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:57.340
The second is how do you
do operational agility

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:59.890
without introducing
more risk, and third,

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:02.860
how do you do a uniform
security from this?

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:06.140
It's easy for one to think that
microservices are, in fact,

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:08.260
terrible.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:10.730
Monolithic programs
didn't have this problem.

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:13.900
Well, obviously, that's
not the right answer.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:15.970
Microservices are
perfectly fine.

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:18.910
Yes, these three are
an issue, and this

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:21.370
is exactly why Istio exists.

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:24.490
Istio is an open
services platform

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:28.480
to manage your service
interactions on containers

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.280
and VM-based workloads.

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:32.270
That second part
is super important.

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:34.659
It's also for
VM-based workloads.

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:36.669
And I mentioned
service interactions.

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:39.250
That's exactly what
a service mesh is--

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:42.070
managing the interactions
of your network

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:45.320
of your microservices
is what Istio does.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:47.440
What do I mean by management?

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:50.170
And that's what we'll
dig deeper into as we

00:08:50.170 --> 00:08:52.990
get into what Istio does.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:55.570
So it still solves the
three problems I [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:58.480
earlier when you're
dealing with microservices.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:01.390
Gives you observability.

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:06.100
Second, you can have
operational agility, i.e.

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:10.780
the ability to introduce
changes quickly into your system

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:13.060
without introducing
additional risk.

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:16.870
And third, you can introduce
policies for security

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:20.200
without having to depend
on individual teams

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:22.450
to adopt those policies.

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:24.910
You can, as an
enterprise standard,

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:27.040
apply broad security
rules and not

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:30.870
expect individual teams to apply
them on every microservice.

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:33.024
So this is a quick
value proposition

00:09:33.024 --> 00:09:35.110
for what Istio does.

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:39.980
Now what we're going to look
at is to see how Istio does it.

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:45.190
The basic architecture construct
for Istio looks like this.

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:48.245
At this point, I'll introduce
the concept of a [INAUDIBLE]..

00:09:48.245 --> 00:09:51.190
Now you'll see here I've
got the services A and B,

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:54.160
and both have a proxy
associated with it.

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:57.940
That proxy is what we
refer to as a sidecar.

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:00.970
A quick definition here
of what a sidecar is--

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:06.550
a sidecar is a process that
assists a parent process where

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:11.540
the parent process can transfer
responsibility to the sidecar.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:15.190
So in this case, service A is
a microservice I've written.

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:18.940
And instead of the microservice
or the microservice developer

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:23.350
implementing every
bit of the [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:23.350 --> 00:10:25.150
the microservice
developer implementing

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:29.020
every bit of the architecture,
the microservice developer

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:32.600
is offloading certain
responsibilities

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:35.620
to the proxy, the sidecar proxy.

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:39.280
Now you must assume as
if the sidecar proxy

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:41.350
is part of your application.

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:43.960
If the sidecar proxy is on
your microservices [INAUDIBLE],,

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:46.700
it really is a one
to one mapping.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:49.510
And most of the times,
they share a kernel

00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:51.170
if you're putting it on VMs.

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:53.290
And if not on VMs,
they're in the same pod

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:54.490
if it's Kubernetes.

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:57.490
So for all practical
purposes, that sidecar proxy

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:59.290
is part of your application.

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:03.070
That microservices team must
treat that sidecar proxy

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:05.440
as if it were part
of that microservice.

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:09.400
It is only then can you
rely on the sidecar proxy

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:12.700
to do certain or take up
certain responsibilities instead

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:16.100
of the individual
microservice doing it.

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:20.240
So what responsibilities can
I delegate to the sidecar?

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:23.360
So first off, there
are two things

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:26.780
that a microservice
does very often.

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:29.540
It invokes other
microservices, or it

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:33.100
gets invoked by other services.

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:36.030
So these are easily broken
down into two aspects--

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:37.870
ingress or an egress.

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:40.250
Service A calls other
services or other services

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:42.750
call service A.

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:47.490
What happens with Istio is that
the sidecar proxy intercepts

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:53.370
all ingress or outbound and all
ingress or all inbound traffic

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:54.760
to the microservice.

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:56.490
So the microservice
itself is never

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:59.490
directly accessible without
going through the sidecar

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:00.450
proxy.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:02.460
And this is a fundamental point.

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:05.220
When you have the
sidecar proxy associated

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:08.430
with every one of your
services, you quickly

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:11.430
realize that all
your network traffic

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:13.800
is happening between
two sidecars,

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:16.780
never happens between the
applications themselves.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:20.250
And this is a fundamental
point to understand

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:24.540
as to how Istio operates and how
it is able to achieve the value

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:28.740
propositions we talked about--
i.e. observability, agility,

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:30.900
and security.

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:34.010
So now that have established
that all inbound and outbound

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:36.020
services go through
the sidecar, we

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:40.580
will now start talking about
what is we do and how we do it.

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:43.430
So assisting the sidecar
are three components.

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:46.100
I'm going to briefly
touch upon them now,

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:48.440
but we are still going
to have a slide that

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:51.510
explains individually
what these components do.

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:52.460
The first is pilot.

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:54.200
Pilot helps with
service discovery.

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:57.170
I have a [INAUDIBLE],, and
we'll talk about it then.

00:12:57.170 --> 00:13:00.455
And the second is a policy
decision point, which is mixer.

00:13:00.455 --> 00:13:03.440
It enforces policies
in the service smash.

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:05.480
And unfortunately, you
can't see the cycle here,

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:07.240
but the last one is citadel.

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:10.460
Citadel will help with security.

00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:13.190
Citadel is a [INAUDIBLE]
certificate authority

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:15.320
so you can achieve
mutual [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:18.280
between your service.

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:20.160
Let's start with security first.

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:22.140
So we have established
the basic premise

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:24.840
that there is a sidecar in
front of all your microservices,

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:28.840
and that sidecar has some
delegated responsibilities.

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:30.390
Let's start with security.

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:34.030
How does security
happen with Istio?

00:13:34.030 --> 00:13:36.670
There are three
important security points

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:38.820
that Istio provides.

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:41.670
The first is
enabling mutual TLS.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:43.830
That is authentication
and encryption

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:45.140
between your services.

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:47.730
And this is a very
fundamental point.

00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:52.520
Individual applications no
longer have to implement TLS.

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:54.643
[INAUDIBLE]
microservice in service

00:13:54.643 --> 00:13:56.060
A [INAUDIBLE]
microservice service

00:13:56.060 --> 00:14:01.580
B need to have absolutely no
knowledge of how to do TLS.

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:05.660
That responsibility
of negotiating TLS

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:09.730
is offloaded to [INAUDIBLE]
or the sidecar proxy.

00:14:09.730 --> 00:14:13.610
By doing this, I have now
implemented a standard way

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:16.310
of achieving TLS
regardless of whether it's

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:19.550
a database, regardless whether
it's a document application

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:20.840
or a Java application.

00:14:20.840 --> 00:14:26.130
That is one way to do mTLS, and
that's still using the sidecar.

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:28.590
The second is to
authorize access

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:31.900
based on service identity,
another crucial and critical

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:32.400
point.

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:36.440
And I have a slide about
it, but I'll catch up to.

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:40.350
The sidecar-- or in
Istio, every microservice

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:42.090
has a service identity.

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:43.840
And we'll come to
that in a second.

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:47.100
And then I can apply
authorization rules

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:48.550
on those services.

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:51.660
So for example, I can say
that only service A can access

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:53.730
service B and no other service.

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:57.480
That is achievable
because we identify

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:00.570
the workload of the service
with an identity, a service

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:01.260
identity.

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:05.120
And that can be used
to apply authorization.

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:10.310
Lastly, you can also apply some
very fine-grained RPC controls.

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:14.300
For example, I want to allow
service A to access service B,

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:17.510
but it can only access
the get operations

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:20.660
and nothing else for only the
post operations and nothing

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:21.240
else.

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:25.970
And so it can also be a very
fine grained organization.

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:31.240
So let's take a quick look
at what these options mean

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:32.740
in detail.

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:35.740
So of course, mutual TLS
is a very standard practice

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:39.350
for securing communication
between two services.

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:41.800
Mutual TLS, we all
use it for encryption

00:15:41.800 --> 00:15:43.630
of messages in transit.

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:47.810
But there's some more
we can do with this.

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:49.850
What Istio provides
is this ability

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:51.770
called a strong authentication.

00:15:51.770 --> 00:15:54.890
And I touched upon
it briefly before.

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:58.490
Istio provides the ability
to create a service identity.

00:15:58.490 --> 00:16:01.000
Now how do we do this?

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:05.010
So there is a standard
called Spiffe, S-P-I-F-F-E,

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:06.930
for those of you
who are interested.

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:09.810
And Citadel is a
certificate authority

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:13.860
that is responsible for signing
certificates in the standard.

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:16.890
So when service A is
brought up, service A

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:19.470
gets a unique service identity.

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:23.280
No other service can pretend
to be service A. I can uniquely

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:26.730
identify service A, and
this is extremely useful

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:30.090
to authenticate that my
service is, in fact, coming

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:32.460
from service A and
not someone else.

00:16:32.460 --> 00:16:35.800
So it's easy for me to have
a rule in service B to say,

00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:41.340
I will only access communication
from service A. In order for me

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:44.940
to do that, I must have
a strong way of achieving

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:47.730
the identity that is
service A, and this

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:50.930
is done through
Spiffe-based certificate.

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:54.830
A second very critical point
is how these certificates

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:57.080
are automatically rotated.

00:16:57.080 --> 00:17:00.200
So every 30 minutes in Istio,
a new T and cert [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:03.830
is generated, and this new NT
cert is issued to service A.

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:06.740
And these certificates
keep rotating.

00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:09.040
In my past life, I
remember we struggled a lot

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:10.630
when it came to
certificate upgrades

00:17:10.630 --> 00:17:12.950
with these giant
monolithic applications

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:15.069
with the Java key
stores and everything.

00:17:15.069 --> 00:17:18.509
We took, developers, at times,
down time to [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:18.509 --> 00:17:20.085
upgrade these certificates.

00:17:20.085 --> 00:17:21.460
That would be a
thing of the past

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:23.290
when you use Istio
because certificates

00:17:23.290 --> 00:17:27.069
are rotated all the time every
30 minutes and automatically.

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:31.350
The second component I want
to introduce here is mixer.

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:34.140
Mixer is that
extensibility point

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:39.090
that allows you to make
policy decisions in Istio.

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:41.603
Mixer integrates with Apigee.

00:17:41.603 --> 00:17:43.020
Mixer integrates
with Stackdriver.

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:46.050
Mixer integrates with Prometheus
and [INAUDIBLE] and all

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:47.580
these other systems.

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:50.230
So Mixer has two main goals.

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:53.490
The first is that it collects
telemetry information

00:17:53.490 --> 00:17:55.810
from the Istio architecture.

00:17:55.810 --> 00:17:57.820
We talked about how
there's a sidecar in front

00:17:57.820 --> 00:17:59.440
of every application.

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:02.920
This presents us with some very
interesting options in addition

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:04.480
to mTLS.

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:07.420
These sidecars can
emit events every time

00:18:07.420 --> 00:18:09.250
there's an ingress or an egress.

00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:12.370
Those events can be captured
by centralized systems

00:18:12.370 --> 00:18:16.180
to produce placing information,
monitoring information,

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:19.030
so you get observability
without changing

00:18:19.030 --> 00:18:21.880
any of your applications
regardless of the code

00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:23.620
that's written in.

00:18:23.620 --> 00:18:28.480
Mixer is that centralized
case that the telemetry events

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:30.230
are selected.

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:32.450
But Mixer also has
another role to play.

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:34.730
Mixer is a policy
decision point.

00:18:34.730 --> 00:18:37.730
This is where you can
hold your authorization

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:40.550
rules to say I want only
service A to access B,

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:44.610
or I want service A to only have
read access to B, and so on.

00:18:44.610 --> 00:18:48.320
These are authorization rules
or policy decision rules

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:51.740
that I can encode into mixer,
and those decisions are then

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:53.710
enforced by the sidecar.

00:18:53.710 --> 00:18:56.510
So mixer provides
that extensibility

00:18:56.510 --> 00:19:01.450
to add those custom
rules into Istio.

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:05.100
The last and final
component here is Pilot.

00:19:05.100 --> 00:19:10.360
Pilot is responsible for
configuring the plane.

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:11.780
So Pilot has two jobs.

00:19:11.780 --> 00:19:14.560
One is providing service
discovery information

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:16.540
when you have a new
service that is registered.

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:19.600
Pilot knows about it and lets
the sidecars know about it.

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:21.280
And second, if
you're doing things

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:25.360
like [INAUDIBLE] releases, AB
testing, all of these rules

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:28.890
are floated into Pilot, and
Pilot pushes that information

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:30.040
to service A.

00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:34.090
So in this case, if I had
a new version of service B

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:39.010
and I want to send 20% of my
traffic to this new service,

00:19:39.010 --> 00:19:43.660
then here is an example of a
YAML file of how I could do it.

00:19:43.660 --> 00:19:46.930
80% of my traffic goes
to the existing service,

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:49.510
20% goes to my new service.

00:19:49.510 --> 00:19:51.250
And this is a very
critical point

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:54.310
in maintaining
operational agility.

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:56.560
Right, we talked
about two things.

00:19:56.560 --> 00:19:58.060
We talked about observability.

00:19:58.060 --> 00:19:59.380
We talked about security.

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:00.740
And this is the third one.

00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:04.360
This is how we introduced
operational agility.

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:06.460
This allows development
teams to iterate

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:09.590
very fast because when you
do introduce a new version,

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:12.250
you don't have to use
100% of your traffic

00:20:12.250 --> 00:20:13.340
to this new version.

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:16.220
You could start with 5%,
test if there are errors,

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:18.580
and if there are errors,
easily switch back

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:20.080
to the previous version.

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:22.630
If there are no errors,
continue to increase traffic

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:24.040
to this newer version.

00:20:24.040 --> 00:20:26.080
What does Istio do in summary?

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:29.800
The value proposition for Istio
is that managing microservices

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:31.340
can be complicated.

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:34.180
It presents a challenge
to production operators

00:20:34.180 --> 00:20:37.540
of how to get observability,
how to introduce

00:20:37.540 --> 00:20:40.300
operational agility
without increasing risk,

00:20:40.300 --> 00:20:43.150
and third, how to do
policy-driven security

00:20:43.150 --> 00:20:46.600
so you're not depending on
individual teams to do it.

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:49.360
And Istio addresses
these concerns

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:52.780
on behalf of the application
or the microservices provider

00:20:52.780 --> 00:20:56.110
by baking these features into
the basic infrastructures.

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:59.170
Istio can do the routing,
Istio can do the security,

00:20:59.170 --> 00:21:01.990
and Istio can introduce
the observability

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:04.280
for what is happening
in your infrastructure.

00:21:04.280 --> 00:21:06.760
So at this point, what
they're going to do is

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:08.735
I'm going to get
my colleague Scott.

00:21:08.735 --> 00:21:11.980
Scott Ganyo is going to
do a quick demo of Istio

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:13.748
and is going to show
you how Istio works.

00:21:13.748 --> 00:21:15.040
SCOTT GANYO: Thank you, Nanden.

00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:16.960
What I've put together here is--

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:17.980
it's a storefront.

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:24.430
So I'm going to operate now
at this point as the operator

00:21:24.430 --> 00:21:29.050
of my store here, and I'm pretty
satisfied with what I've got.

00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:34.630
I can go in and buy a
typewriter, edit my cart,

00:21:34.630 --> 00:21:35.410
view the cart.

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:39.400
I've got all of the
standard capabilities

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:41.470
that you would expect
in the storefront.

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:45.590
Now this whole thing
has been created

00:21:45.590 --> 00:21:49.280
as a series of microservices.

00:21:49.280 --> 00:21:53.630
Now all of this is actually
deployed in a Kubernetes

00:21:53.630 --> 00:21:58.220
environment, and each
of these services

00:21:58.220 --> 00:22:02.240
is developed in different
style, different language.

00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:04.640
We have everything
from Python to Java

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:11.600
to Go represented here as
well as database information

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:17.330
and accessing external
services as well.

00:22:17.330 --> 00:22:23.430
So the issue here, then, of
course, as Nandan talked about,

00:22:23.430 --> 00:22:26.740
is how do we manage all of this.

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:28.710
Well, the first thing
I wanted to show you

00:22:28.710 --> 00:22:32.250
is very simple thing but
a very powerful thing,

00:22:32.250 --> 00:22:41.460
and that is being able to
route that information as

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:45.150
like a Canary-type
implementation.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:51.090
And so Nanden showed you
a version of the YAML.

00:22:51.090 --> 00:22:53.250
This is a slightly
different version.

00:22:53.250 --> 00:23:00.020
And my shop is a lot of gray.

00:23:00.020 --> 00:23:01.280
That's my color.

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:04.890
But I've heard now that
blue is the hip color.

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:07.520
And so what I've done
is I've rolled out

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:10.940
or I've deployed a
version of my app

00:23:10.940 --> 00:23:15.230
that takes into account
the fact that I've

00:23:15.230 --> 00:23:17.670
heard that people like blue.

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:22.870
So what you see here is I've
change the virtual service,

00:23:22.870 --> 00:23:25.580
and I've set up a destination.

00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:30.100
So subsets of 80% of
the requests go to gray,

00:23:30.100 --> 00:23:33.196
and 20% go to blue.

00:23:33.196 --> 00:23:36.555
So once I apply that--

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:41.590
so we're configured now.

00:23:41.590 --> 00:23:46.960
Now whenever we go through here,
you see every once in a while,

00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:48.960
we'll get a blue page.

00:23:48.960 --> 00:23:50.890
Oops, not on that page.

00:23:50.890 --> 00:23:57.750
But my header is going to be
either gray or blue depending

00:23:57.750 --> 00:23:58.550
on how we hit it.

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:03.670
So that's cool.

00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:08.980
That means I can test that
small change in an AB way

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:12.720
or in a Canary-type rollout.

00:24:12.720 --> 00:24:16.080
And I can easily
get back from it

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:19.470
because it turns out that
my customers actually really

00:24:19.470 --> 00:24:21.200
preferred the gray.

00:24:21.200 --> 00:24:25.100
And I can just roll that back
with another quick change where

00:24:25.100 --> 00:24:29.630
I set the 100% of my
traffic back to gray.

00:24:29.630 --> 00:24:35.380
And I will never see that
blue abomination again.

00:24:38.242 --> 00:24:42.020
So that's a quick example
of how you actually

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:44.838
use the routing capabilities.

00:24:44.838 --> 00:24:46.880
Another thing that I want
to show you real quick,

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:48.830
though, is some
of the monitoring.

00:24:48.830 --> 00:24:52.790
If we jump in, we have
full telemetry information

00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:54.110
and tracing information.

00:24:54.110 --> 00:24:57.950
And this is the
Jaeger UI, and it

00:24:57.950 --> 00:25:01.480
shows you the requests that
have been coming through.

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:04.660
And I can actually drill
down into one of these

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:07.990
and see exactly
where all the time is

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:11.290
being spent, like
what's being called,

00:25:11.290 --> 00:25:13.400
and how long it's taking.

00:25:13.400 --> 00:25:19.400
So if I need to get information
about the actual operations

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:23.050
of what's going on,
I can very easily

00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:26.710
figure out where I need
to make optimizations

00:25:26.710 --> 00:25:31.000
or I need to make
changes to fix things.

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:35.130
The other thing I wanted
to show you is Grafana.

00:25:35.130 --> 00:25:39.330
And if you look, we can
get a very detailed view

00:25:39.330 --> 00:25:43.020
of the information
about the system,

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:47.280
of the memory, CPU, all
the information about that.

00:25:47.280 --> 00:25:53.830
I can also show you a service
dashboard, front end service.

00:25:53.830 --> 00:25:58.570
And it shows you request volume,
success rate, things like that.

00:25:58.570 --> 00:26:00.670
So we have very detailed
information about what's

00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:01.900
going on in the system.

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:04.960
And all this is just
absolutely built into Istio

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:09.450
and available at all
times and can be extended

00:26:09.450 --> 00:26:13.760
as well various plug-ins.

00:26:13.760 --> 00:26:16.812
So I'm going to hand this
back to Nanden, then.

00:26:16.812 --> 00:26:18.520
NANDEN SRIDHAR: So
what you have seen now

00:26:18.520 --> 00:26:23.050
is how to use Istio to do
basic service management.

00:26:23.050 --> 00:26:25.600
Like, what you've
seen with service

00:26:25.600 --> 00:26:29.980
management needs are the
basic ability to do mTLS.

00:26:29.980 --> 00:26:33.130
So you can encrypt and
authenticate your traffic.

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:35.890
Second, you want to have
this operational agility

00:26:35.890 --> 00:26:37.550
so you can do AB testing.

00:26:37.550 --> 00:26:40.040
You can [INAUDIBLE]
releases, all of that.

00:26:40.040 --> 00:26:42.610
And third, you want to
do policy enforcement.

00:26:42.610 --> 00:26:46.250
That applies to all services.

00:26:46.250 --> 00:26:47.520
And this is back to back.

00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:50.580
So how does service
management really

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:53.060
tie in with API management?

00:26:53.060 --> 00:26:56.990
So first, let us establish
what is API management,

00:26:56.990 --> 00:27:00.680
and then let's talk about how
service management compares

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:02.000
with API management.

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:07.970
API management is the ability
to provide an API catalog

00:27:07.970 --> 00:27:09.200
for discovery.

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:11.590
If you go back to this
architecture here--

00:27:11.590 --> 00:27:14.250
this is the application
that Scott wrote--

00:27:14.250 --> 00:27:15.830
it's got a currency service.

00:27:15.830 --> 00:27:17.980
It's got a check out service.

00:27:17.980 --> 00:27:20.650
Very frequently in
enterprises, the team

00:27:20.650 --> 00:27:22.120
that built the
currency service is

00:27:22.120 --> 00:27:25.660
different from the team that
built the check out service.

00:27:25.660 --> 00:27:28.810
Now that teams that built
the currency service

00:27:28.810 --> 00:27:33.010
will very frequently want
to have exposed that service

00:27:33.010 --> 00:27:36.478
in some sort of a catalog that's
available in the enterprise.

00:27:36.478 --> 00:27:38.770
And the team that built the
checkout service will refer

00:27:38.770 --> 00:27:41.980
to the currency service's API
specification, potentially

00:27:41.980 --> 00:27:43.550
request access to it.

00:27:43.550 --> 00:27:46.757
And if access should be
granted by the team that

00:27:46.757 --> 00:27:49.090
built the currency service,
they will grant that access,

00:27:49.090 --> 00:27:51.360
and then we go
ahead and build it.

00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:54.620
So while as a service to
service communication path,

00:27:54.620 --> 00:27:58.160
none of this was visible, at
the development stages, when

00:27:58.160 --> 00:28:01.880
these services were in fact
being shared between teams,

00:28:01.880 --> 00:28:05.140
API management was
definitely needed.

00:28:05.140 --> 00:28:07.290
So let's [INAUDIBLE] that.

00:28:07.290 --> 00:28:10.380
If I'm just building services
that is only ever going

00:28:10.380 --> 00:28:13.830
to be consumed from within
my team, then by all means,

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:15.930
something like a Java
doc or a readme file

00:28:15.930 --> 00:28:17.730
is good enough to
share information

00:28:17.730 --> 00:28:21.200
about how to consume
that other service.

00:28:21.200 --> 00:28:22.970
But the minute you
want your service

00:28:22.970 --> 00:28:28.490
to be available outside your
team into a consumable form,

00:28:28.490 --> 00:28:31.070
then you want to have a
catalog to publish in.

00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:33.950
The moment you have a catalog
where people can consume

00:28:33.950 --> 00:28:36.260
your services, you want
to have the ability

00:28:36.260 --> 00:28:40.350
to control who has access and
how they have access to it

00:28:40.350 --> 00:28:42.410
and so on.

00:28:42.410 --> 00:28:48.210
The next step is the ability
to view usage reporting.

00:28:48.210 --> 00:28:50.990
You saw Scott show a
distributed tree which

00:28:50.990 --> 00:28:53.410
said this is how
much latency is spent

00:28:53.410 --> 00:28:55.850
in every single
microservice and so on.

00:28:55.850 --> 00:28:59.960
But neither [? Grafana ?] nor
the [? Jaeger ?] UI showed

00:28:59.960 --> 00:29:04.700
Scott who his top biggest five
applications are because Istio

00:29:04.700 --> 00:29:06.770
does not have the view
of an application.

00:29:06.770 --> 00:29:09.170
It has view of services,
and many services

00:29:09.170 --> 00:29:11.280
form an application.

00:29:11.280 --> 00:29:13.010
For example, your call
center application

00:29:13.010 --> 00:29:16.910
isn't one big application, but
it is a bunch of microservices.

00:29:16.910 --> 00:29:18.600
But if you want
that view of what

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:21.560
does it look like from an
application level, which

00:29:21.560 --> 00:29:23.870
is a logical grouping
of microservices,

00:29:23.870 --> 00:29:27.520
you want to have some
analytics being reported.

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:31.000
If you want to
productize your data

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:34.150
and expose it to others
for monetization purposes,

00:29:34.150 --> 00:29:36.610
very likely you're not going
to productize individuals

00:29:36.610 --> 00:29:38.650
microservices, but you're
going to productize

00:29:38.650 --> 00:29:41.200
a logical grouping of
these microservices

00:29:41.200 --> 00:29:44.565
and make it available
as a single API.

00:29:44.565 --> 00:29:46.690
And lastly, of course, you
want to have the ability

00:29:46.690 --> 00:29:51.400
to do documentation, try
your APIs, and so on.

00:29:51.400 --> 00:29:54.310
Then there is the aspect
in an API gateway.

00:29:54.310 --> 00:29:56.500
Within an API gateway,
I want the ability

00:29:56.500 --> 00:29:58.360
to do transformations
and mediations.

00:29:58.360 --> 00:30:01.180
I want the ability to do
proctoring and quotas.

00:30:01.180 --> 00:30:04.630
I want set protection for
my JSON and XML payloads,

00:30:04.630 --> 00:30:07.510
and I want some caching
optimization techniques

00:30:07.510 --> 00:30:08.690
to be available.

00:30:08.690 --> 00:30:10.630
These are certain
crosscutting systems

00:30:10.630 --> 00:30:12.790
that you want in a
gateway so that again,

00:30:12.790 --> 00:30:17.270
individual microservice
developers don't have to do it.

00:30:17.270 --> 00:30:19.440
So keeping these
in mind, let's now

00:30:19.440 --> 00:30:23.700
compare API management
and service management.

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:29.230
All APIs are services but
not all services are APIs.

00:30:29.230 --> 00:30:33.730
Your caching service is a
service, but it's not an API.

00:30:33.730 --> 00:30:37.530
Your website is a service,
but it's not an API.

00:30:37.530 --> 00:30:39.880
But the invoicing
service is also

00:30:39.880 --> 00:30:43.120
an API because I could
invoke it as an API.

00:30:43.120 --> 00:30:47.050
So we think that Istio
and service management

00:30:47.050 --> 00:30:48.880
is needed for all services.

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:53.230
Anything with at least a TCP
port can be managed by Istio.

00:30:53.230 --> 00:30:54.550
That could be in your database.

00:30:54.550 --> 00:30:58.270
That would mean your NoSQL
database, whatever that may be.

00:30:58.270 --> 00:31:00.880
And when some of
those services need

00:31:00.880 --> 00:31:04.450
to be exposed for consumption
outside of your team,

00:31:04.450 --> 00:31:08.410
then it becomes an API, and
you need API management for it.

00:31:08.410 --> 00:31:11.560
And that's how we try to look
at API management and service

00:31:11.560 --> 00:31:12.340
management.

00:31:12.340 --> 00:31:14.920
They are really
complementary functions.

00:31:14.920 --> 00:31:17.500
Even if you drill down
into the features offered

00:31:17.500 --> 00:31:21.950
by both of them, you'll notice
that although the features

00:31:21.950 --> 00:31:26.210
section look similar, they
offer different perspectives.

00:31:26.210 --> 00:31:30.650
API management of course
works on HTTP-based APIs,

00:31:30.650 --> 00:31:34.550
but service management can
apply to a TCP-based service,

00:31:34.550 --> 00:31:36.640
and that's perfectly fine.

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:39.310
When it comes to authorization
and authentication,

00:31:39.310 --> 00:31:43.280
service management relies on
mutual TLS and the identity

00:31:43.280 --> 00:31:45.700
in the Spiffe
certificate for identity

00:31:45.700 --> 00:31:50.140
whereas API management can rely
on API keys and OAuth tokens

00:31:50.140 --> 00:31:53.860
and not necessarily
at the nTLS level.

00:31:53.860 --> 00:31:55.360
When you look at
monitoring, it's

00:31:55.360 --> 00:31:58.780
really monitoring from a
production operations team

00:31:58.780 --> 00:31:59.760
point of view.

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:02.590
It's a production ops person who
wants to know the distributed

00:32:02.590 --> 00:32:05.920
trace to find bottlenecks.

00:32:05.920 --> 00:32:09.670
Apigee's monitoring is more
from APIs provider point of view

00:32:09.670 --> 00:32:11.320
where you're going
to try and take it

00:32:11.320 --> 00:32:14.180
as to which application
that's consuming these

00:32:14.180 --> 00:32:17.590
is causing the most errors,
who are my top 10 developers,

00:32:17.590 --> 00:32:22.670
and you see how this [INAUDIBLE]
almost leads into analytics.

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:24.320
Rate limiting is
also very different.

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:26.520
A rate limiting for
service management

00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:29.570
is more to do with I want
to protect my back end.

00:32:29.570 --> 00:32:32.250
But a rate limiting from an
API management point of view

00:32:32.250 --> 00:32:34.010
is from the consumer
point of view

00:32:34.010 --> 00:32:37.220
to say I don't want
these consumers to exceed

00:32:37.220 --> 00:32:38.330
their quota.

00:32:38.330 --> 00:32:40.010
I want to create a gold tier.

00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:41.720
I want to create
a platinum tier.

00:32:41.720 --> 00:32:44.700
I want to create a
bronze tier and so on.

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:46.550
These are all consumer-specific.

00:32:46.550 --> 00:32:49.790
They are not backend-specific.

00:32:49.790 --> 00:32:53.960
Lastly, monetization and
portals are abilities

00:32:53.960 --> 00:32:55.940
that come with APIs management.

00:32:55.940 --> 00:32:57.860
You don't really
monetize services.

00:32:57.860 --> 00:33:00.590
You monetize APIs.

00:33:00.590 --> 00:33:03.350
So I hope you're drawing
this distinction of how

00:33:03.350 --> 00:33:05.540
service management
and API management

00:33:05.540 --> 00:33:07.460
are complementary features.

00:33:07.460 --> 00:33:10.160
Service management is needed
for all your services.

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:12.950
And when some of those
services become APIs,

00:33:12.950 --> 00:33:16.040
you use API management.

00:33:16.040 --> 00:33:20.780
So how do we Apigee
integrate with Istio?

00:33:20.780 --> 00:33:23.310
Assuming that you've
already got Istio

00:33:23.310 --> 00:33:27.800
to do services management, then
how do we layer on top of this

00:33:27.800 --> 00:33:30.390
to also do API management?

00:33:30.390 --> 00:33:32.760
What you're seeing here
is the architecture

00:33:32.760 --> 00:33:35.180
that we have for
services management.

00:33:35.180 --> 00:33:36.650
It includes the side car.

00:33:36.650 --> 00:33:40.400
It includes mixer and pilot,
and I have on purpose not

00:33:40.400 --> 00:33:41.810
introduced Citadel here.

00:33:41.810 --> 00:33:44.030
It's not relevant to this
current conversation.

00:33:44.030 --> 00:33:45.640
But it could have been here.

00:33:45.640 --> 00:33:47.970
So put service management here.

00:33:47.970 --> 00:33:50.880
In my previous slide,
we talked about how

00:33:50.880 --> 00:33:53.530
mixer is that
extensibility point

00:33:53.530 --> 00:33:55.530
that you would have kept it.

00:33:55.530 --> 00:33:59.370
Naturally, we would extend
mixer to add API management

00:33:59.370 --> 00:34:01.170
capability.

00:34:01.170 --> 00:34:03.030
In addition to
that, we introduced

00:34:03.030 --> 00:34:04.890
two important concepts--

00:34:04.890 --> 00:34:09.000
the concept of an API portal and
the concept of an API dashboard

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:10.469
to view the metrics.

00:34:10.469 --> 00:34:12.929
And mixer becomes the
policy decision point

00:34:12.929 --> 00:34:15.750
to do API key or
OAuth verification.

00:34:15.750 --> 00:34:18.000
It's the point where you
do quota enforcement,

00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:21.480
and it's also the place where
you collect analytics to send

00:34:21.480 --> 00:34:25.070
it off to the dashboard.

00:34:25.070 --> 00:34:28.010
The next point is how
does Apigee do it.

00:34:28.010 --> 00:34:32.840
Well, naturally, we integrate
the mixer component of Istio

00:34:32.840 --> 00:34:37.010
to talk to Apigee Edge or the
control plane in Apigee Edge

00:34:37.010 --> 00:34:40.620
so that the Istio
mixer is Apigee aware.

00:34:40.620 --> 00:34:42.830
And this is a very
critical point.

00:34:42.830 --> 00:34:46.340
You have now, the integration
of mixer to Apigee edge,

00:34:46.340 --> 00:34:50.690
you have now made Istio
Apigee API management aware.

00:34:50.690 --> 00:34:52.940
Mixer is now able
to enforce the rules

00:34:52.940 --> 00:34:54.830
that you define in Apigee.

00:34:54.830 --> 00:34:58.070
So Apigee becomes a place
where you author entitlements.

00:34:58.070 --> 00:35:01.070
Apigee becomes a place where
you publish your services

00:35:01.070 --> 00:35:02.180
for your portal.

00:35:02.180 --> 00:35:05.330
And those rules are
pulled down by mixer

00:35:05.330 --> 00:35:09.240
and then enforced
in the service mesh.

00:35:09.240 --> 00:35:12.000
So let's take this
with an actual example.

00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:16.130
We have the retail site
Fazzio that [INAUDIBLE]..

00:35:16.130 --> 00:35:18.350
And now [INAUDIBLE]
got a new requirement.

00:35:18.350 --> 00:35:21.560
This new requirement
is the shipping service

00:35:21.560 --> 00:35:24.200
needs to be exposed externally.

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:25.910
And that's the
requirement he had.

00:35:25.910 --> 00:35:27.980
So far, the shipping
service was only

00:35:27.980 --> 00:35:30.560
used by the checkout
service, and that was never

00:35:30.560 --> 00:35:33.800
exposed outside, and it was
all done through the front end

00:35:33.800 --> 00:35:34.790
application.

00:35:34.790 --> 00:35:37.730
But now his requirement
is to actually expose

00:35:37.730 --> 00:35:39.470
the shipping service as an API.

00:35:39.470 --> 00:35:41.593
So Scott, tell us
how you do this.

00:35:41.593 --> 00:35:42.260
SCOTT GANYO: OK.

00:35:42.260 --> 00:35:43.490
Thanks, Nanden.

00:35:43.490 --> 00:35:45.500
So what we're going
to do, first off,

00:35:45.500 --> 00:35:48.050
is expose our new tracking API.

00:35:48.050 --> 00:35:51.020
We have a service,
and we already

00:35:51.020 --> 00:35:55.450
have a gateway that's exposing
the application itself.

00:35:55.450 --> 00:35:59.870
But what we need down here
is to expose the tracking

00:35:59.870 --> 00:36:05.210
and route it to our
shipping microservice

00:36:05.210 --> 00:36:08.390
and allow access through that.

00:36:08.390 --> 00:36:13.620
So once we apply this,
then we will have access.

00:36:13.620 --> 00:36:15.470
It's configured.

00:36:15.470 --> 00:36:19.730
We will have access into
this API as you can see here.

00:36:23.320 --> 00:36:27.970
The next step that we're going
to look at is wiring up Apigee.

00:36:27.970 --> 00:36:34.330
So we need to be able to apply
Apigee analytics to our Istio

00:36:34.330 --> 00:36:38.100
routing or to our
Istio application,

00:36:38.100 --> 00:36:40.860
and we also want to
apply authorization.

00:36:40.860 --> 00:36:43.470
Right now, I'm going to
show you a little bit of how

00:36:43.470 --> 00:36:44.970
that wiring happens.

00:36:44.970 --> 00:36:48.030
The Apigee Analytics,
as you can see here,

00:36:48.030 --> 00:36:54.000
this little CRD basically
tells the Istio mixer

00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:58.290
which attributes in
Istio to send to Apigee.

00:36:58.290 --> 00:37:01.545
And you can see we have requests
times and destination hosts

00:37:01.545 --> 00:37:02.420
and things like that.

00:37:02.420 --> 00:37:04.260
So this is the analytics part.

00:37:04.260 --> 00:37:07.200
And we also have a
template for authorization

00:37:07.200 --> 00:37:13.080
that tells mixer which parts of
the authorization, when it does

00:37:13.080 --> 00:37:16.000
authorization, which
attributes from mixer

00:37:16.000 --> 00:37:19.000
need to go to Apigee so
that we can do that as well.

00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:21.870
And here you'll see
things like API key,

00:37:21.870 --> 00:37:26.530
and you'll see claims from
OAuth and things like that.

00:37:26.530 --> 00:37:31.950
So in both cases, we're
able to report on not just

00:37:31.950 --> 00:37:34.185
the request itself but
who's making the request.

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:41.450
The next thing that
we'll do is apply a rule.

00:37:41.450 --> 00:37:45.920
Now this is what actually tells
mixer that we want to send--

00:37:45.920 --> 00:37:47.540
in this case,
we're going to send

00:37:47.540 --> 00:37:52.220
all inbound requests
to the Apigee adapter

00:37:52.220 --> 00:37:53.750
and thus to Apigee.

00:37:53.750 --> 00:37:55.230
And this is for analytics.

00:37:55.230 --> 00:37:58.280
So we'll take everything
that there is.

00:37:58.280 --> 00:37:59.540
Configure that rule.

00:37:59.540 --> 00:38:02.194
And now we can look at
the analytics reporting.

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:07.020
Now when you get into
Apigee analytics,

00:38:07.020 --> 00:38:10.260
it gives you all kinds
of great information.

00:38:10.260 --> 00:38:16.590
This, for example, shows by
device type, but you can also--

00:38:16.590 --> 00:38:22.720
you can drill in on this
one to various services.

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:25.860
So we can look at the
product catalog service

00:38:25.860 --> 00:38:29.760
and how it's being hit.

00:38:29.760 --> 00:38:32.340
We can look at
error code analysis

00:38:32.340 --> 00:38:35.220
and actually see if
we're getting errors.

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:38.160
And we're not getting many.

00:38:38.160 --> 00:38:41.220
We can look at
developer engagement

00:38:41.220 --> 00:38:43.890
is interesting once we
have a set of developers.

00:38:43.890 --> 00:38:48.720
And we'll talk about that in
just a moment and then actually

00:38:48.720 --> 00:38:52.260
API proxy performance as well.

00:38:52.260 --> 00:38:53.780
So you can see all
the developers,

00:38:53.780 --> 00:38:57.530
and you can be able
to sort them by email.

00:38:57.530 --> 00:39:00.430
So you can see that.

00:39:00.430 --> 00:39:05.980
OK, so-- and we have all
that coming into Apigee now.

00:39:05.980 --> 00:39:11.940
But now we need to expose what
we're doing to the developers.

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:14.260
And we do that
through a product.

00:39:14.260 --> 00:39:16.990
And when we create
an API product,

00:39:16.990 --> 00:39:23.890
this is what actually gives us
the control over how the API is

00:39:23.890 --> 00:39:26.900
accessed by developers.

00:39:26.900 --> 00:39:29.320
So what you see here is I've
created a product called

00:39:29.320 --> 00:39:31.990
tracking details,
and I've made it

00:39:31.990 --> 00:39:35.530
publicly available to
any registered developer.

00:39:35.530 --> 00:39:39.020
So once they've registered,
they can have access to this,

00:39:39.020 --> 00:39:41.440
and it's automatic approval.

00:39:41.440 --> 00:39:42.700
I set a quota.

00:39:42.700 --> 00:39:47.470
So any developer can actually
only access this once or--

00:39:47.470 --> 00:39:50.940
sorry, five times-- each minute.

00:39:50.940 --> 00:39:54.450
I'm not setting any
OAuth scopes right now.

00:39:54.450 --> 00:39:57.030
I set a path of
slash, which basically

00:39:57.030 --> 00:39:59.500
means they have
access to anything,

00:39:59.500 --> 00:40:03.460
any paths on this
particular product.

00:40:03.460 --> 00:40:07.960
And then the interesting
part here is Istio services.

00:40:07.960 --> 00:40:10.750
Now I've bound this to
this particular service

00:40:10.750 --> 00:40:13.840
that I've already deployed
and that I opened up access

00:40:13.840 --> 00:40:15.110
to a second ago.

00:40:15.110 --> 00:40:16.990
And this is the
shipping service.

00:40:16.990 --> 00:40:20.140
And this is the full
DNS name of the shipping

00:40:20.140 --> 00:40:24.060
service in Kubernetes.

00:40:24.060 --> 00:40:29.860
So having set all
that, let me save that.

00:40:29.860 --> 00:40:33.160
And we come back, and
now we can register

00:40:33.160 --> 00:40:34.960
to use that API in the portal.

00:40:37.580 --> 00:40:42.080
So I come to the portal
and check out the APIs.

00:40:42.080 --> 00:40:43.700
Oh, there's my API.

00:40:43.700 --> 00:40:44.840
It's the tracking details.

00:40:44.840 --> 00:40:49.040
And I've already exposed
this to the portal

00:40:49.040 --> 00:40:57.620
with an open API definition
as you can see here.

00:40:57.620 --> 00:41:00.710
If you look at this
open API definition,

00:41:00.710 --> 00:41:05.160
we have a get tracking, and
you pass it a tracking number.

00:41:05.160 --> 00:41:09.300
And the body of a
response is going

00:41:09.300 --> 00:41:12.810
to have information such as the
carrier, the status, tracking

00:41:12.810 --> 00:41:14.620
location, things like that.

00:41:14.620 --> 00:41:17.190
And then you can see here we
also have the authentication

00:41:17.190 --> 00:41:19.770
requirements, that it
requires either an API

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:24.252
key or a jwt_bearer token.

00:41:24.252 --> 00:41:25.960
So right now, we don't
have it protected.

00:41:25.960 --> 00:41:31.470
So we should be able to access
it with no additional work.

00:41:31.470 --> 00:41:33.700
And there we go.

00:41:33.700 --> 00:41:37.560
So we get a correct response.

00:41:37.560 --> 00:41:38.720
Now that's great.

00:41:38.720 --> 00:41:41.090
I can try the API.

00:41:41.090 --> 00:41:45.320
But what I really want
to do is create an app

00:41:45.320 --> 00:41:48.950
so that I can use the
API as a developer

00:41:48.950 --> 00:41:51.200
because normally, you're
not going to have the AP

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:54.950
open to just anybody, right?

00:41:54.950 --> 00:42:00.220
So what we're going to
do is protect the API

00:42:00.220 --> 00:42:01.360
with an API key.

00:42:04.260 --> 00:42:08.510
So now I've said that my
API is no longer public.

00:42:08.510 --> 00:42:10.640
It has to be a
registered developer

00:42:10.640 --> 00:42:13.290
because you have to have
an API key to access this.

00:42:13.290 --> 00:42:16.430
And you'll notice that
we're saying it's any--

00:42:16.430 --> 00:42:20.510
we're only doing it for
the shipping destination.

00:42:20.510 --> 00:42:23.810
And that's so that our app can
continue working as normal,

00:42:23.810 --> 00:42:25.200
the web app.

00:42:25.200 --> 00:42:28.410
And this only affects that
shipping API or the shipping

00:42:28.410 --> 00:42:30.530
service, in fact.

00:42:30.530 --> 00:42:34.370
So let's go back to
the portal again.

00:42:34.370 --> 00:42:36.250
Let's go here.

00:42:36.250 --> 00:42:40.060
And now we should
see if we execute--

00:42:40.060 --> 00:42:43.820
yes, we have a 403
permission denied.

00:42:43.820 --> 00:42:49.070
Now we have to have an
API key to access it.

00:42:49.070 --> 00:42:52.160
And we can do that
ourselves as well.

00:42:52.160 --> 00:42:56.020
But what we have to do is we
create an account and sign in.

00:42:56.020 --> 00:42:57.520
Now I've already
created an account.

00:43:00.655 --> 00:43:02.280
So I'm going to log
in with my account.

00:43:06.050 --> 00:43:10.960
And if we go here, you can see
that I don't have any apps.

00:43:10.960 --> 00:43:13.100
Well, I have to create an app.

00:43:13.100 --> 00:43:15.325
So I'm going to
call it my details.

00:43:20.540 --> 00:43:24.230
Now once the app
is created, you'll

00:43:24.230 --> 00:43:31.270
see that I have access or
I grant access to this API

00:43:31.270 --> 00:43:33.940
and add an API key for the app.

00:43:38.920 --> 00:43:44.330
Now this should be able
to be used directly

00:43:44.330 --> 00:43:47.490
to try out that API.

00:43:47.490 --> 00:43:51.560
So let's go back to this API.

00:43:51.560 --> 00:43:54.890
So now we're going to
authorize this with the API key

00:43:54.890 --> 00:43:57.030
that we just created.

00:43:57.030 --> 00:44:00.420
And actually, let me
manually enter it.

00:44:00.420 --> 00:44:01.950
I'll show you the key.

00:44:01.950 --> 00:44:12.040
Authorize, and we get a 200
OK, and we're back in business.

00:44:12.040 --> 00:44:12.990
Excellent.

00:44:12.990 --> 00:44:15.240
So API key is working for us.

00:44:21.300 --> 00:44:25.070
So now we need to
try this with OAuth.

00:44:25.070 --> 00:44:30.830
First of all, we'll go ahead
and protect it with the OAuth.

00:44:30.830 --> 00:44:35.780
And you can see here we have
an auth spec that tells it

00:44:35.780 --> 00:44:39.620
we're using my issuer and
where I get the tokens

00:44:39.620 --> 00:44:44.930
and where to retrieve
my certs from.

00:44:44.930 --> 00:44:48.710
In this case, we'll go
ahead and apply that.

00:44:48.710 --> 00:44:55.820
Now when we apply that, that
actually goes out to the proxy.

00:44:55.820 --> 00:44:58.190
So it's actually
running on the envoy.

00:44:58.190 --> 00:45:00.200
And envoy retrieves
that information

00:45:00.200 --> 00:45:04.240
and can evaluate
that right there.

00:45:04.240 --> 00:45:06.870
So let's go back to
the portal once again.

00:45:10.650 --> 00:45:14.097
Then we'll try it with
the same authorization

00:45:14.097 --> 00:45:14.930
that we have before.

00:45:19.080 --> 00:45:24.125
And this time, we get origin
authentication failed.

00:45:24.125 --> 00:45:26.625
It's a different error because
that's coming from the proxy.

00:45:29.670 --> 00:45:32.000
So now what we need to
do is we need to go back,

00:45:32.000 --> 00:45:37.090
and we have to get a token.

00:45:37.090 --> 00:45:41.300
So to get the token, again,
go back into our app.

00:45:46.660 --> 00:45:49.250
And we're going to need
the key and the secret.

00:45:49.250 --> 00:45:51.680
So let me grab the key.

00:45:51.680 --> 00:45:55.010
And I'm just going
to go ahead and put

00:45:55.010 --> 00:45:59.051
these in here, the secret.

00:45:59.051 --> 00:46:04.330
We'll put that in
here, evaluate that,

00:46:04.330 --> 00:46:08.640
and then see if we can
create this token for us.

00:46:08.640 --> 00:46:11.970
Here's the token
that it's created.

00:46:11.970 --> 00:46:15.220
And this is the actual contents
of what that token looks like.

00:46:15.220 --> 00:46:17.280
And you can see that we
have all the information

00:46:17.280 --> 00:46:19.960
about the user in this.

00:46:19.960 --> 00:46:24.180
So let's grab this
token and go ahead

00:46:24.180 --> 00:46:31.470
and put it in the bare
header in this access.

00:46:31.470 --> 00:46:35.790
And you can see now
we have access again.

00:46:35.790 --> 00:46:37.950
And I'll turn this
back over to Nandan.

00:46:37.950 --> 00:46:38.550
Thank you.

00:46:38.550 --> 00:46:41.520
NANDEN SRIDHAR: What you see
now is the ability for us

00:46:41.520 --> 00:46:45.710
to extend the Istio mixer
adaptor to apply API management

00:46:45.710 --> 00:46:47.530
tools in a service mesh.

00:46:47.530 --> 00:46:51.540
So service management
provides the value

00:46:51.540 --> 00:46:55.500
of applying observability,
operational agility,

00:46:55.500 --> 00:46:57.570
and security to
your microservices.

00:46:57.570 --> 00:47:00.720
Apigee extends service
management in Istio

00:47:00.720 --> 00:47:04.560
to give you the ability to have
a portal, to get API analytics,

00:47:04.560 --> 00:47:08.940
and then to apply API management
rules within your service mesh

00:47:08.940 --> 00:47:11.730
like quotas, API key
verification, OAuth,

00:47:11.730 --> 00:47:13.620
and all those things
that you typically

00:47:13.620 --> 00:47:17.820
associate with API management.

